WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.384
Picture an evening with a thick recipe book.

2
00:00:05.384 --> 00:00:10.081
You read the first recipe and ask: can I make this with what's in the fridge?

3
00:00:10.081 --> 00:00:11.606
No, so you turn the page.

4
00:00:11.606 --> 00:00:14.960
At the first yes, you close the book and start cooking.

5
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.925
Nobody checks every recipe first, and then picks the first match.

6
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:22.716
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:22.716 --> 00:00:25.349
A chain of map and filter reads so nicely.

8
00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:27.668
Can it do more work than I asked for?

9
00:00:27.668 --> 00:00:29.673
And what does asSequence change?

10
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.144
Here's the recipe app.

11
00:00:32.144 --> 00:00:34.460
Its book holds a million recipes.

12
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:42.811
To pick tonight's dinner, it asks a matching service, a program on another computer, to rate each recipe from 0 to 100.

13
00:00:42.811 --> 00:00:45.688
Each question takes about 2 milliseconds.

14
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.683
The search is a chain from the last lesson.

15
00:00:48.683 --> 00:00:54.767
map gives each recipe its score, filter keeps 90 or more, and firstOrNull takes the first one.

16
00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:57.291
It reads well, and the answer is right.

17
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:04.224
But the first good match is recipe number 518, and this chain still asks about all million.

18
00:01:04.224 --> 00:01:09.471
That's about 33 minutes, for an answer that was ready after one second.

19
00:01:09.471 --> 00:01:10.431
Right answer.

20
00:01:10.431 --> 00:01:11.244
Wrong bill.

21
00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:12.887
Here's why.

22
00:01:12.887 --> 00:01:15.242
On a List, map and filter are eager.

23
00:01:15.242 --> 00:01:22.768
Each step loops over everything, writes every result into a brand new list, and only then does the next step start.

24
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.485
Let's shrink it to one tray, with our cookies from the last lesson.

25
00:01:28.485 --> 00:01:32.270
Five cookies, c1 to c5, and c1, c3 and c5 are golden.

26
00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.269
We want two golden cookies for a lunchbox.

27
00:01:35.269 --> 00:01:39.983
So: filter the golden ones, map each one to its grams, and take 2.

28
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.576
Each lambda prints a line when it runs.

29
00:01:42.576 --> 00:01:45.440
On a List, in what order do the lines come out?

30
00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:52.081
filter runs on all five cookies first.

31
00:01:52.081 --> 00:01:54.486
Then map runs on c1, c3 and c5.

32
00:01:54.486 --> 00:01:58.520
Eight calls, and the lunchbox holds 45 and 50 grams.

33
00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.941
Poor c5 got weighed for a lunchbox that was already full.

34
00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:06.372
Now picture a kitchen line with two helpers.

35
00:02:06.372 --> 00:02:08.208
One checks each cookie's color.

36
00:02:08.208 --> 00:02:09.571
The next one weighs it.

37
00:02:09.571 --> 00:02:14.545
With a list, the checker goes through the whole tray before the weigher even starts.

38
00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:22.890
With a sequence, the first cookie walks down the whole line, checked and then weighed, before the second cookie leaves the tray.

39
00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.521
And when the lunchbox is full, the line stops.

40
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:27.691
Let's map it.

41
00:02:27.691 --> 00:02:29.129
A cookie is one item.

42
00:02:29.129 --> 00:02:32.280
The two helpers are the steps, filter and map.

43
00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.527
The lunchbox is take 2, and toList is the one who asks for it.

44
00:02:36.527 --> 00:02:40.295
The cookies left on the tray are items nobody looks at.

45
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.905
Same chain, with asSequence in front.

46
00:02:43.905 --> 00:02:45.075
What prints now?

47
00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:52.180
First: built TakeSequence, nothing has run yet.

48
00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.193
Then filter c1, map c1.

49
00:02:54.193 --> 00:02:56.118
filter c2, not golden.

50
00:02:56.118 --> 00:02:57.693
filter c3, map c3.

51
00:02:57.693 --> 00:03:00.931
Five calls, and the lunchbox is full.

52
00:03:00.931 --> 00:03:03.381
c4 and c5 are never touched.

53
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.452
Look at that first line again.

54
00:03:05.452 --> 00:03:07.612
The sequence is in no hurry at all.

55
00:03:07.612 --> 00:03:12.057
It wrote down the plan, and it will wait all evening for someone to ask.

56
00:03:12.057 --> 00:03:15.637
So the point really is: nothing runs until something asks.

57
00:03:16.567 --> 00:03:17.526
Now the names.

58
00:03:17.526 --> 00:03:24.925
A sequence is a lazy chain of steps: no work until a result is wanted, and then only what that result needs.

59
00:03:24.925 --> 00:03:29.858
map and filter are intermediate operations: they add a step to the plan.

60
00:03:29.858 --> 00:03:35.751
toList, first or sum are terminal operations: they ask for items, and the work starts.

61
00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:39.819
Back to the recipe book, and count the calls.

62
00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:43.181
The List chain asks the service a million times.

63
00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:45.913
The sequence asks 518 times, and stops.

64
00:03:45.913 --> 00:03:50.606
Stopping as soon as the answer is known is called short circuiting.

65
00:03:50.606 --> 00:03:55.580
And firstOrNull with a lambda is one plain loop that also stops at 518.

66
00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:57.563
How does it stop?

67
00:03:57.563 --> 00:04:01.690
Each step on a sequence is a small wrapper around the step before.

68
00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:06.817
filter makes a FilteringSequence, map a TransformingSequence, take a TakeSequence.

69
00:04:06.817 --> 00:04:12.444
toList asks the last wrapper for an item, and it asks the one before it, back to the tray.

70
00:04:12.444 --> 00:04:15.008
One cookie gets pulled up the whole line.

71
00:04:15.933 --> 00:04:21.405
Here's the heart of the map wrapper in Kotlin, next to the same idea by hand in Java.

72
00:04:21.405 --> 00:04:25.589
Get the next item from the step before, and run the lambda on it.

73
00:04:25.589 --> 00:04:31.383
Java's stream works the same way, one item at a time, and its demo prints the same counts.

74
00:04:31.383 --> 00:04:35.245
The price: lambdas are objects again, and numbers get boxed.

75
00:04:36.167 --> 00:04:38.050
Now let's break the picture.

76
00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:40.539
Put sortedBy in front of firstOrNull.

77
00:04:40.539 --> 00:04:42.760
The service still runs 518 times.

78
00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:46.796
But how many recipes does the sequence pull out of the book?

79
00:04:49.767 --> 00:04:50.688
All 1,000,000.

80
00:04:50.688 --> 00:04:58.848
sortedBy can't hand out the smallest item until it has seen every item, so it pulls the whole book into a list and sorts it.

81
00:04:58.848 --> 00:05:03.060
No helper can tell the lightest cookie by looking at one cookie.

82
00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.799
If you only need the smallest, minByOrNull reads once and sorts nothing.

83
00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.098
So, should every chain be a sequence?

84
00:05:11.098 --> 00:05:11.292
No.

85
00:05:11.292 --> 00:05:16.477
For a short chain over a few hundred items, a list is fine, and easier to debug.

86
00:05:16.477 --> 00:05:22.374
Reach for a sequence when the input is big, a step is slow, or you need only the first few.

87
00:05:22.374 --> 00:05:25.096
And put the step that can stop at the end.

88
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.151
So, can a nice chain do more work than you asked for?

89
00:05:29.151 --> 00:05:32.124
On a List, yes: each step finishes the whole tray.

90
00:05:32.124 --> 00:05:37.177
A sequence sends one cookie down the whole line, and stops when the lunchbox is full.

91
00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:38.666
Quick check.

92
00:05:38.666 --> 00:05:42.240
In the List chain, move take 2 up, right after the tray.

93
00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.005
How many filter lines print, and what goes in the lunchbox?

94
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.387
Two filter lines, c1 and c2, and the lunchbox holds only 45.

95
00:05:53.387 --> 00:05:57.116
That's a different question, so a different answer.

96
00:05:57.116 --> 00:06:00.113
Laziness only changes how much work runs.
